WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.261
Some AWS CLI actions are higher impact
because they can change security posture,

2
00:00:06.341 --> 00:00:10.662
data exposure, availability, or recovery
options quickly.

3
00:00:10.742 --> 00:00:15.910
IAM permission changes, network rule
changes, storage policy updates,

4
00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:20.650
encryption setting changes, compute
changes, logging changes,

5
00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:26.915
and data-access changes deserve stronger
guardrails than simple read-only queries.

6
00:00:26.995 --> 00:00:30.384
The script should make those actions
explicit.

7
00:00:30.464 --> 00:00:36.310
Where AWS services or deployment workflows
support defensive preview behavior,

8
00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:36.898
use it.

9
00:00:36.978 --> 00:00:40.875
Dry-run checks, policy simulation, diffs,
previews,

10
00:00:40.955 --> 00:00:46.462
and change sets can help reviewers
understand the effective change before it

11
00:00:46.542 --> 00:00:47.220
happens.

12
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.638
These tools are not a substitute for least
privilege or target validation,

13
00:00:52.718 --> 00:00:58.225
but they provide a safer review point
before a high-impact operation reaches

14
00:00:58.305 --> 00:00:59.237
production.

15
00:00:59.317 --> 00:01:03.892
Deletion, overwrite, public exposure,
permission expansion,

16
00:01:03.972 --> 00:01:07.107
and encryption changes deserve extra care.

17
00:01:07.187 --> 00:01:12.778
A helper should require target validation,
release context, approval gates,

18
00:01:12.858 --> 00:01:19.043
operator confirmation, or change tickets
when the operation could affect production

19
00:01:19.123 --> 00:01:20.902
or sensitive resources.

20
00:01:20.982 --> 00:01:24.710
It should also avoid ambiguous resource
selection.

21
00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:29.958
If a script cannot prove exactly which
target is in scope, it should stop.

22
00:01:30.038 --> 00:01:36.308
Guardrails should support idempotent
behavior, rollback thinking, and safe failure.

23
00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:42.149
An operation should be designed so
repeated runs do not create uncontrolled side

24
00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:42.907
effects.

25
00:01:42.987 --> 00:01:47.647
Recovery options should be considered
before the change begins,

26
00:01:47.727 --> 00:01:49.845
not after impact is detected.

27
00:01:49.925 --> 00:01:55.940
Good AWS CLI automation makes high-impact
actions visible, reviewed, and bounded.
